 "article": {
  "title": "What Maps Hide",
  "topic": "人文历史（地图的选择与偏见）",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "words": 159,
  "paras": [
   "Every map is an argument. It selects, simplifies and ranks, and in doing so it tells you what to notice.",
   "The Mercator projection, still common on classroom walls, stretches Greenland until it looks the size of Africa. It is excellent for navigation and misleading for almost everything else. A generation of students grew up with a distorted picture of the world and no note in the corner explaining why.",
   "Digital maps hide things differently. They optimise for the fastest route, so a street with a school on it looks identical to an empty one. They rank by popularity, so a well-reviewed restaurant is visible and a better one two streets away is not.",
   "The remedy is not to abandon maps but to read them. Ask who made it, what it was made for, and what has been left out. A map that admits its purpose is a tool. A map that pretends to be the world is a trap."
  ],
  "zh": "每张地图都是一个主张：它做选择、做简化、做排序，并因此告诉你该注意什么。教室里常见的墨卡托投影把格陵兰放大到看起来和非洲一样大——它适合导航，却几乎在别的所有方面误导人，一代学生带着扭曲的世界图像长大，角落里却没有一行字解释原因。数字地图用另一种方式隐藏：它优化“最快路线”，于是有学校的街道和空街看起来一模一样；它按热度排序，于是点评多的餐馆显眼，两条街外更好的一家却看不见。解决办法不是不要地图，而是会读地图：问是谁做的、为什么做、漏掉了什么。承认自己用途的地图是工具，假装自己就是世界的地图是陷阱。",
  "hard": [
   {
    "en": "It is excellent for navigation and misleading for almost everything else.",
    "cn": "它非常适合导航，却几乎在所有其他方面误导人。",
    "why": "excellent for 与 misleading for 共用介词 for，形成一正一反的平衡结构，省略重复成分让句子更紧凑。"
   },
   {
    "en": "A map that admits its purpose is a tool. A map that pretends to be the world is a trap.",
    "cn": "承认自己用途的地图是工具；假装自己就是世界的地图是陷阱。",
    "why": "两句完全对称，都用 that 定语从句修饰 map，pretend to be 意为“自称是”。这种对偶句适合放在段落结尾当警句。"
   }
  ],
